The token is verified against the JWKS of the core named in its `iss` — every
regional core listed in `ENTIRE_TRUSTED_ISSUERS` (`issuer=jwksURL,…`), so a
caller whose home region mints its tokens elsewhere authenticates here; an
issuer outside that list is rejected before any signature work. With the list
unset the cell falls back to `CORE_JWKS_URL` alone (its own region only). The
account access token's audience is that of the issuer it was dispatched to, so
each trusted core admits its own. `home_jurisdiction` rides on both shapes, so
`/me` reads it directly — no per-request Core `/me` call.

| Method | Path | Returns |
|--------|------|---------|
| GET | `/me` | identity + jurisdictions (drives cross-region federation) |
| GET | `/me/commits` | my commits in `?timeframe`/`?since`/`?until` |
| GET | `/me/checkpoints` | my checkpoints in window + `streakDates` |
| GET | `/me/activity` | daily buckets + per-repo + per-agent + streak; `?repo_ids=` narrows |
| GET | `/me/recap` | per-agent aggregates + summary; `?repo=` adds team column (federated if foreign) |

The table above is the `/me/*` surface (user store). All of it is served by the
single **api** process, alongside the repo aggregates below. The product
surface's OpenAPI document is merged with the cell's other services into one
served spec (at the gateway root, e.g. `/openapi.yaml`); its operations also
remain available directly under `/api/v1` with docs at `/api/v1/docs`. Health:
`/healthz`, `/readyz` (the workers expose these too, with no API surface).

The api process also serves the repo / contributor / project **aggregates**
(metadata + pointers only — content via the Repo API). A repository — and the
project that owns it — is hosted in a **single jurisdiction** (a project's repos
are co-located with it), so each aggregate is **complete within that region**.
Authz is fail-closed: the caller must have read access (`403` denied / `503` on
Core error), and a repo not in this region returns `404`.

| Method | Path | Returns |
|--------|------|---------|
| GET | `/repos/{id}/summary` | headline rollup (commits, checkpoints, contributors, tokens, lines, active days) |
| GET | `/repos/{id}/activity` | time buckets, `?granularity=day\|week` (UTC) |
| GET | `/repos/{id}/contributors` | leaderboard by author email, `?sort=commits\|checkpoints\|tokens\|lines` |
| GET | `/repos/{id}/agents` | per-agent checkpoint rollup |
| GET | `/repos/{id}/commits` | commit metadata, keyset-paginated (`?cursor`) |
| GET | `/repos/{id}/checkpoints` | checkpoint metadata, keyset-paginated |
| GET | `/repos/{id}/checkpoints/{cid}` | one checkpoint's metadata + linked commit shas |
| GET | `/projects/{id}/summary` | project rollup (complete; the project's region) |
| GET | `/projects/{id}/repos` | per-repo rollup within the project |
| GET | `/projects/{id}/contributors` | project contributor leaderboard |
| GET | `/projects/{id}/activity` | project time buckets |

Aggregates are **commit-anchored** (keyed on `committed_at` + `author_email`,
matching `/me`); checkpoint metrics join via the commit-trailer link, repo/
project totals count `DISTINCT` checkpoints. A project — and every repo it owns —
is hosted in a **single region**, so a project's region serves a complete view;
there is no cross-region union for projects.

**Commit lifecycle state.** Every commit is `shipped` (reachable from the default
branch), `in_progress` (on a branch, not yet on the default), or `orphaned`
(reachable from no branch — rebased / force-pushed away). Counts **exclude
orphaned** and **split shipped vs in-progress** on both the team aggregates and
`/me`; `/me` streaks stay state-agnostic, so a later rebase never erases a worked
day. State is ref-derived on the index path (the pushed ref vs the default
branch); a force-push or branch delete triggers a reclassify pass that re-checks
reachability via the Repo API.

## Architecture

The three processes are deployed **once per jurisdiction** (US, EU, AU, SG, …)
over two stores: the repo-activity worker owns the **repo store**
(`REPO_DATABASE_DSN`) for repos that live here; the user-activity worker owns the
**user store** (`USER_DATABASE_DSN`) of residency-safe per-author metadata
forwarded in from wherever the repos live; the api process reads both to serve
the HTTP surface. The workers communicate only over NATS (repo-activity's publisher
→ user-activity's consumer).

There is **no NATS supercluster** — each cluster runs its own NATS island.
Cross-region delivery is a **direct publish into the recipient's home-cluster
NATS**: the forwarder holds one connection per cluster (keyed by `cluster_id`,
from `ACTIVITY_NATS_REGIONS` + `ACTIVITY_LOCAL_CLUSTER_ID`) and publishes into
`conns[homeCluster]`, mirroring mirror-pipeline's `cmd/fanout`. Residency is
enforced by *which connection* the event goes to, not by post-hoc filtering.

### Deployment topology

![Deployment topology: two jurisdictions (US repo, EU author) with the cross-region forward](docs/topology.svg)

